\title{Dependency-Adjusted Fitness of Mathematical Theories}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{6 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop an exact, reproducible cost model for comparing bodies of mathematics that prove the same finite corpus of statements. A \emph{theory} is recorded by the transitive dependency closure of the material it uses together with the corpus statements it settles; its \emph{dependency-adjusted cost} is the total source length of that closure, each item charged exactly once, and its \emph{fitness} is the number of corpus statements proved per unit cost.

We prove that the cost model is canonical: the transitive dependency closure of a base set exists inside any finite universe and is the least dependency-closed superset of that base; dependency-closed collections are closed under union and intersection, so the shared material of two developments is itself a legitimate development; and merging obeys the exact inclusion--exclusion identity $\cost(T\sqcup U)+\text{shared}=\cost(T)+\cost(U)$. On a fixed corpus, fitness is a purely ordinal inverse of cost, so every nonempty finite comparison class has a champion. For a fixed proof system the champion is canonical --- the transitive closure of the corpus' proof bases is a global fitness maximum over the entire unbounded class of dependency-closed covering developments, unique up to cost; with two inequivalent proof routes canonicity provably fails, while existence of a minimum-cost cover survives. We establish an exact $k$-fold reuse identity $\cost(\text{library})+k\cdot\corebase=\sum_i\cost(\text{specialist}_i)+\corebase$; a sharp composition phase transition at adapter cost equal to shared dependency mass, in absolute and scale-free density form, together with a multiplicative-corpus criterion under which candidate growth dominates additive cost; and exact candidate counts --- dependency-closed subsets multiply across independent splits, an independent family of $n$ items admits exactly $2^n$ usable sub-libraries, a chain exactly $n+1$, with strict collapse for $n\ge 2$. Finally we describe the landscape: every semantics-preserving migration across an interface boundary contains a state overshooting the smaller endpoint length by at least $(\alpha-\beta)/(1+\beta)$; stylewise-optimal developments with style-closed neighbourhoods are strict local maxima, invariantly under renaming, and a computed nine-development landscape has three distinct strict local maxima of which only one is global; and in any language admitting conservative inflation at sublinear marginal source cost, raw theorem-per-line fitness is unbounded with semantically inert witnesses, giving a sharp dichotomy in which resource normalisation is the decisive hypothesis.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} dependency closure; theory fitness; reuse; phase transition; fitness landscape; local maxima; set cover; conservative extension.

\section{Introduction}

Fix a finite list of mathematical statements --- a \emph{corpus} --- and consider the many bodies of mathematics that prove all of them. Some are terse and specialised; some erect an abstract framework after which every statement is a corollary. Practitioners argue about which style is better, and the argument never resolves, because ``better'' has never been given a definition sharp enough to admit a proof.

This paper supplies such a definition and determines the resulting structure. It has three ingredients: a \emph{corpus}, fixing what counts as an achievement; a \emph{cost model}, fixing what counts as an expenditure; and a rule for \emph{charging shared material}, fixing when two developments have done the same work. The third is delicate, and is what \emph{dependency-adjusted} refers to. Once all three are fixed, much folklore becomes provable --- and a specific part of it becomes provably false without further hypotheses.

Contributions: a canonical cost model based on least dependency-closed supersets (\S\ref{sec:cost}); the ordinal reduction of fitness to cost and the finite maximum principle (\S\ref{sec:ordinal}); the global champion theorem with a matched counterexample and a survival result (\S\ref{sec:champion}); the exact $k$-fold reuse identity (\S\ref{sec:reuse}); the composition phase transition (\S\ref{sec:composition}); exact candidate counts (\S\ref{sec:counts}); the quantitative adapter valley and three-style metastability (\S\ref{sec:landscape}); and non-existence of a normalisation-free global maximum (\S\ref{sec:unbounded}).

\section{The cost model}\label{sec:cost}

Mathematical items --- definitions, lemmas, theorems, notations --- are indexed by natural numbers; all collections are finite.

\subsection{Dependency closure}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{dependency structure} assigns to each item $i$ a finite set $\deps(i)$ of items, its \emph{direct dependencies}. A finite set $S$ is \emph{dependency-closed} if $\deps(i)\subseteq S$ for every $i\in S$.
\end{definition}

A dependency-closed set is precisely a self-contained development: one readable linearly without encountering an item whose prerequisites are missing.

\begin{lemma}[Lattice structure]\label{lem:lattice}
If $S$ and $T$ are dependency-closed then so are $S\cup T$ and $S\cap T$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For the union, $i\in S\cup T$ lies in one of the two, whose closedness places $\deps(i)$ inside that set and a fortiori inside the union. For the intersection, $i\in S\cap T$ gives $\deps(i)\subseteq S$ and $\deps(i)\subseteq T$.
\end{proof}

The intersection clause is not decoration: it states that the shared material of two self-contained developments is itself self-contained, which is exactly the licence needed to treat ``shared dependency mass'' as a real object.

\begin{definition}
For a set $S$ put $\stepop(S)=S\cup\bigcup_{i\in S}\deps(i)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:step}
$S\subseteq\stepop(S)$; $\stepop(S)=S$ iff $S$ is dependency-closed; and if $T$ is dependency-closed with $S\subseteq T$ then $\stepop(S)\subseteq T$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{definition}
Fix a finite universe $U$ and a base $B$. Put $\overline{B}=\stepop^{\,|U|+1}(B)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Canonicity of the closure]\label{thm:closure}
Suppose $B\subseteq U$ and $U$ is dependency-closed. Then $B\subseteq\overline{B}\subseteq U$, $\overline{B}$ is dependency-closed, and (\emph{minimality}) $\overline{B}\subseteq T$ for every dependency-closed $T$ with $B\subseteq T$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Inclusion in $U$ and containment of $B$ follow by induction on the number of rounds, using Lemma~\ref{lem:step}; minimality is the same induction with $T$ in place of $U$. Closedness is the substantive point: the iterates form an increasing chain inside $U$, so if no fixed point were reached within $|U|+1$ rounds each round would strictly increase cardinality, forcing $|\stepop^{|U|+1}(B)|\ge|U|+1>|U|$, contradicting containment in $U$. Hence the chain stabilises and the round-$(|U|+1)$ value is a fixed point of $\stepop$, i.e.\ dependency-closed by Lemma~\ref{lem:step}.
\end{proof}

Minimality is what makes the model canonical: $\overline{B}$ is the unique smallest self-contained development containing $B$, so there is no slack in charging a development for its prerequisites.

\subsection{Theories, cost, fitness}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{theory} $T$ consists of a finite set $C(T)$ of items (its transitive dependency closure) and a finite set $P(T)$ of corpus statements it proves. Given a source-length function $\ell:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$,
\[
\cost_\ell(T)=\sum_{i\in C(T)}\ell(i),\qquad
\fit_\ell(T)=\frac{|P(T)|}{\cost_\ell(T)}\in\mathbb{Q}.
\]
The \emph{merge} $T\sqcup U$ has $C(T\sqcup U)=C(T)\cup C(U)$ and $P(T\sqcup U)=P(T)\cup P(U)$.
\end{definition}

Each item in the closure is charged once, however many times it is used; this is the entire content of ``dependency-adjusted''.

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity]\label{lem:mono}
If $C(T)\subseteq C(U)$ then $\cost_\ell(T)\le\cost_\ell(U)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Exact merge accounting]\label{thm:merge}
\[
\cost_\ell(T\sqcup U)+\sum_{i\in C(T)\cap C(U)}\ell(i)=\cost_\ell(T)+\cost_\ell(U).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Summation over a union plus summation over the intersection equals the sum of the separate summations, each item of the union being counted once for each set containing it.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\cost_\ell(T\sqcup U)\le\cost_\ell(T)+\cost_\ell(U)$: merging never costs more than duplicating.
\end{corollary}

\section{Fitness is ordinally inverse to cost}\label{sec:ordinal}

\begin{theorem}[Ordinal reduction]\label{thm:ordinal}
If $|P(T)|=|P(U)|>0$ and both costs are positive, then
$\fit_\ell(T)\le\fit_\ell(U)\iff\cost_\ell(U)\le\cost_\ell(T)$, and likewise with both inequalities strict.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With a common positive numerator $n$, the map $x\mapsto n/x$ is strictly decreasing on the positive rationals.
\end{proof}

On a fixed corpus nothing matters beyond its cardinality: comparing fitness reduces to comparing two natural numbers.

\begin{theorem}[Finite maximum principle]\label{thm:finmax}
For any nonempty finite index set $F$ and family of theories $(T_a)_{a\in F}$ there is $b\in F$ with $\fit_\ell(T_a)\le\fit_\ell(T_b)$ for all $a\in F$.
\end{theorem}

Trivial as mathematics, this plays a load-bearing methodological role: existence of a champion is free, so all the content lies in identification --- and, as \S\ref{sec:unbounded} shows, the finiteness hypothesis cannot be dropped.

\begin{theorem}[Shared library is champion, abstract form]\label{thm:sharedchamp}
Let $(T_a)_{a\in F}$ satisfy $|P(T_a)|=|P(L)|>0$ and $C(L)\subseteq C(T_a)$ for all $a$, with $\cost_\ell(L)>0$. Then $\fit_\ell(T_a)\le\fit_\ell(L)$ for all $a\in F$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Lemma~\ref{lem:mono} then Theorem~\ref{thm:ordinal}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Champion characterisation]
Under the same hypotheses, $T_b$ is a fitness champion of the family iff it has minimal cost in the family.
\end{theorem}

Hence the champion question is empirically decidable by a single measurement per competitor: the total source length of the transitive closure.

\section{The canonical champion, and the boundary of canonicity}\label{sec:champion}

\subsection{Fixed proof systems}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{proof system} consists of a dependency structure together with a function assigning each statement $s$ the set $\operatorname{base}(s)$ of items consumed by its chosen proof. A theory $T$ \emph{proves} $s$ if $\operatorname{base}(s)\subseteq C(T)$ and \emph{covers} a corpus if it proves each statement. For a corpus $K$ inside a universe $U$, the \emph{canonical library} has closure $C_{\mathrm{can}}=\overline{\bigcup_{s\in K}\operatorname{base}(s)}$ and proves $K$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:cancover}
The canonical library covers $K$, and every dependency-closed $T$ covering $K$ satisfies $C_{\mathrm{can}}\subseteq C(T)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each $\operatorname{base}(s)$ lies in the union of the bases, hence in its closure (Theorem~\ref{thm:closure}). Conversely coverage places that union inside $C(T)$, and minimality places its closure inside $C(T)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Dependency-adjusted global champion]\label{thm:globalchamp}
Fix a corpus $K$ with $|K|>0$ and suppose the canonical library has positive cost. Then for every theory $T$ with $P(T)=K$, dependency-closed closure, and covering $K$,
\[
\fit_\ell(T)\le\fit_\ell(\text{canonical library}).
\]
Moreover any such $T$ attaining this fitness has exactly the same cost.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Lemma~\ref{lem:cancover} and Lemma~\ref{lem:mono} give the cost inequality; Theorem~\ref{thm:ordinal} inverts it, and equality of fitness forces equality of cost.
\end{proof}

The quantifier matters: the comparison class is not a finite tournament but the entire class of dependency-closed covering developments. Reuse wins for a structural reason --- by minimality of the closure, every rival must contain the shared core.

\subsection{Alternative routes destroy canonicity}

\begin{theorem}[No canonical champion with two routes]\label{thm:tworoutes}
Consider the corpus $\{s\}$ with developments $R_1$, $R_2$ of closures $\{a\}$, $\{b\}$, $a\ne b$, all items of length $1$, both proving $s$. Then $\fit(R_1)=\fit(R_2)=1$, the closures are incomparable, and $C(R_1)\cap C(R_2)=\varnothing$.
\end{theorem}

So the covering developments have no least element, the intersection of the two optima proves nothing, and the canonical construction has no analogue. What fails is uniqueness --- not existence.

\begin{definition}
A \emph{multi-route proof system} assigns each statement $s$ a finite family $\routes(s)$ of alternative bases. A collection $c$ proves $s$ if $r\subseteq c$ for some $r\in\routes(s)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Champion with alternative routes]\label{thm:mincover}
Let $U$ be a finite universe covering $K$, with $|K|>0$ and all covering subsets of positive cost. Then some $c\subseteq U$ covers $K$ at minimal cost, and it is a fitness maximum among all covering subsets of $U$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:tworoutes} it need not be unique.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The covering subsets form a nonempty finite family (it contains $U$), so the cost attains a minimum; Theorem~\ref{thm:ordinal} converts minimal cost into maximal fitness.
\end{proof}

The transition from Theorem~\ref{thm:globalchamp} to Theorem~\ref{thm:mincover} is a change of \emph{character}: from an optimum computable by a closure operation to an optimum determined by optimisation over an exponential family --- a weighted set-cover instance in disguise.

\section{Exact \texorpdfstring{$k$}{k}-fold reuse accounting}\label{sec:reuse}

Fix a finite index set $F$ of size $k$, a core $\corebase$, and pairwise disjoint private sets $\priv(i)$ each disjoint from the core. The \emph{shared library} has closure $\corebase\cup\bigcup_{i\in F}\priv(i)$; the \emph{specialist} $i$ has closure $\corebase\cup\priv(i)$, having rebuilt the core privately. All prove the same corpus.

\begin{theorem}[Exact reuse identity]\label{thm:reuse}
\[
\sum_{x\in \corebase\cup\bigcup_i\priv(i)}\!\!\ell(x)\;+\;k\sum_{x\in\corebase}\ell(x)
\;=\;\sum_{i\in F}\;\sum_{x\in \corebase\cup\priv(i)}\!\!\ell(x)\;+\;\sum_{x\in\corebase}\ell(x).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By disjointness the library sum splits as $\corebase+\sum_i\priv(i)$ and the specialist sum as $k\cdot\corebase+\sum_i\priv(i)$; substitute and cancel.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict dominance]\label{thm:reusedom}
If $k\ge2$ and the core has positive cost, the shared library is strictly cheaper than the pooled suite of specialists, hence (for a nonempty corpus) strictly fitter:
\[
\frac{|K|}{\sum_{i\in F}\cost_\ell(\text{specialist }i)}<\fit_\ell(\text{shared library}).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:reuse} exhibits the deficit as $(k-1)\cost(\corebase)>0$; the library cost is positive since the core lies in its closure; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:ordinal}.
\end{proof}

The saving grows linearly in the number of clients: this is the formal explanation of why general libraries are net-negative at $k=1$ and decisively positive at large $k$.

\section{The composition phase transition}\label{sec:composition}

\begin{definition}
For theories $T,U$ and adapter charge $A\in\mathbb{N}$: the \emph{shared mass} is $S(T,U)=\sum_{i\in C(T)\cap C(U)}\ell(i)$; the \emph{composed cost} is $\cost_\ell(T\sqcup U)+A$; the \emph{duplicate cost} is $\cost_\ell(T)+\cost_\ell(U)$. Composed and duplicated fitness both have numerator $|P(T)\cup P(U)|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:compacct}
$\text{composed cost}+S(T,U)=\text{duplicate cost}+A$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Composition trichotomy]\label{thm:trichotomy}
Assume the composite corpus is nonempty and both costs positive. Composition strictly increases fitness iff $A<S(T,U)$; is fitness-neutral iff $A=S(T,U)$; strictly decreases fitness iff $A>S(T,U)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With a common positive numerator, comparing fitnesses reverses the comparison of denominators (Theorem~\ref{thm:ordinal}); by Lemma~\ref{lem:compacct} the composed cost is below, equal to, or above the duplicate cost exactly as $A$ is below, equal to, or above $S(T,U)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Density form]
Writing $\rho=S(T,U)/\text{duplicate cost}$ for the dependency density and $\alpha_A=A/\text{duplicate cost}$ for the adapter density, composition strictly increases fitness iff $\alpha_A<\rho$.
\end{theorem}

Both densities are dimensionless and directly measurable, so the criterion transfers between projects of different sizes.

\begin{remark}[A computed instance]
Two developments with closures $\{0,1,2,3\}$ and $\{2,3,4,5\}$, proving $\{0,1\}$ and $\{2,3\}$, with $\ell\equiv 10$: shared mass $20$, duplicate cost $80$, pooled cost $60$. With $A=10$ fitness rises from $4/80$ to $4/70$; with $A=20$ it is unchanged; with $A=30$ it falls to $4/90$. Both phases are realised and the crossing is exact.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Multiplicative growth beats additive cost]\label{thm:mult}
Let $\fit^{\times}=|P(T)|\cdot|P(U)|/\text{composed cost}$ be the fitness of a composite proving the \emph{product} corpus. If $|P(T)|>0$, $\cost_\ell(T)>0$ and
\[
\cost_\ell(T)+\cost_\ell(U)+A<\cost_\ell(T)\cdot|P(U)|,
\]
then $\fit_\ell(T)<\fit^{\times}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The composed cost is at most $\cost(T)+\cost(U)+A$; multiply the hypothesis by $|P(T)|>0$ and chain the inequalities.
\end{proof}

Costs add (at worst); candidates multiply. Any second component with sufficiently large corpus makes composition profitable whatever the adapter charge --- but ``sufficiently large'' is quantified explicitly, and for small $|P(U)|$ the adapter dominates.

\section{Exact candidate counts}\label{sec:counts}

Theorem~\ref{thm:mult} presupposes that combining independent components multiplies the population of achievable results. That premise is verified exactly where the candidates are the \emph{usable sub-libraries}.

\begin{definition}
$N(U)$ denotes the number of dependency-closed subsets of $U$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Independent parts multiply]\label{thm:mulsplit}
If $U=A\cup B$ with $A,B$ disjoint, $\deps(i)\subseteq A$ for $i\in A$, and $\deps(i)\subseteq B$ for $i\in B$, then $N(A\cup B)=N(A)\cdot N(B)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$s\mapsto(s\cap A,\,s\cap B)$ and $(p,q)\mapsto p\cup q$ are mutually inverse: closedness of a union of closed sets is Lemma~\ref{lem:lattice}; $s=(s\cap A)\cup(s\cap B)$ as $s\subseteq A\cup B$; and disjointness gives $(p\cup q)\cap A=p$, $(p\cup q)\cap B=q$. A bijection of finite sets equates cardinalities; the result is exact, not asymptotic.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The two extremes]\label{thm:extremes}
If $\deps(i)=\varnothing$ for all $i$ then $N(U)=2^{|U|}$. If $\deps(0)=\varnothing$ and $\deps(i)=\{i-1\}$ for $i\ge1$, then $N(\{0,\dots,n-1\})=n+1$. Consequently $n+1<2^{n}$ for all $n\ge2$: dependency density strictly collapses the candidate population, and the gap is exponential.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
In the free case every subset is vacuously closed. In the chain case a closed subset is downward closed --- by induction on $d$, if $i\in s$ then $i-d\in s$, since either $i-d=0$ or the closure condition supplies $(i-d)-1$ --- hence is an initial segment; there are exactly $n+1$ initial segments inside $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$, and the map $k\mapsto\{0,\dots,k-1\}$ is injective. The final inequality follows by induction from $3<4$ using $2^{m}+2^{m}=2^{m+1}$.
\end{proof}

This is a genuine design trade-off in exact numbers. Dependencies enable reuse --- they are how a core is shared --- and simultaneously destroy modularity, since each one prunes the family of self-contained fragments extractable in isolation. Between $n+1$ and $2^n$ lies every real library, and its location on that axis is a measurable structural invariant.

\section{The shape of the landscape}\label{sec:landscape}

\subsection{Migration paths and adapter valleys}

\begin{definition}
A state $w$ of a migration is a triple $(\len(w),\iface(w),\content(w))$: source length, an identifier for the principal abstraction layer, and the intrinsic size of the content implemented. A \emph{migration path} is a finite walk $w_0,\dots,w_n$ of bounded refactorings; it is \emph{semantics preserving} if $\content(w_i)=\content(w_0)$ for all $i\le n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Boundary crossing]\label{thm:crossing}
If $\iface(w_0)\ne\iface(w_n)$ then $\iface(w_i)\ne\iface(w_{i+1})$ for some $i<n$.
\end{theorem}

Elementary, but it localises the phenomenon to a single measurable class of transitions rather than to the path as a whole.

\begin{theorem}[Quantitative adapter valley]\label{thm:valley}
Let $w_0,\dots,w_n$ be semantics preserving with content $C>0$ and let $0\le\beta<\alpha$. Assume $\iface(w_0)\ne\iface(w_n)$; that any state from which the interface changes satisfies $\len(w_i)\ge(1+\alpha)C$; and that $\len(w_0)\le(1+\beta)C$. Then for some $i\le n$, with $m=\min\{\len(w_0),\len(w_n)\}$,
\[
\len(w_i)-m\;\ge\;\frac{\alpha-\beta}{1+\beta}\;m .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take the crossing index $i$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:crossing}: then $\len(w_i)\ge(1+\alpha)C$ while $m\le\len(w_0)\le(1+\beta)C$. Since $(\alpha-\beta)/(1+\beta)\ge0$,
\[
\frac{\alpha-\beta}{1+\beta}m\le\frac{\alpha-\beta}{1+\beta}(1+\beta)C=(\alpha-\beta)C\le(1+\alpha)C-(1+\beta)C\le\len(w_i)-m,
\]
the degenerate case $m\le0$ being immediate.
\end{proof}

The overshoot depends only on the two efficiency exponents: not on the path length, the size of the development, or the ingenuity of the refactoring, and it is strictly positive whenever $\beta<\alpha$. Since fitness is inverted cost, a length barrier is a fitness valley: one cannot walk downhill in cost from one convention to another.

\begin{remark}[Worked instance]
Take $w_0=(110,\,\text{iface }0,\,100)$, $w_1=(150,\,\text{iface }0,\,100)$, $w_2=(110,\,\text{iface }1,\,100)$ with $\alpha=1/2$, $\beta=1/10$. The guaranteed relative overshoot is $(0.5-0.1)/1.1=4/11\approx 36.4\%$, and the realised overshoot at $w_1$ is $40/110=4/11$: the bound is attained.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Metastability of methodological styles}

\begin{definition}
Given a fitness function and a neighbourhood relation $\adj$, a state $b$ is a \emph{strict local maximum} if $\fit(t)<\fit(b)$ for every $t\ne b$ with $\adj(b,t)$; given a style map, $b$ is \emph{style-optimal} if $\fit(t)<\fit(b)$ for every $t\ne b$ of the same style.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Style-centre theorem]\label{thm:stylecentre}
If every bounded refactoring preserves style and $b$ is style-optimal, then $b$ is a strict local maximum. More generally it suffices that $b$ be style-optimal and that every cross-style neighbour of $b$ be strictly less fit.
\end{theorem}

The weakened (``quarantine'') hypothesis is the realistic one: boundaries may be crossed provided crossing hurts --- which is exactly what Theorem~\ref{thm:valley} supplies. Crucially the two hypotheses are independently measurable, which is what makes metastability falsifiable on a finite corpus.

\begin{theorem}[Renaming invariance]
Let $\sigma$ be a bijection of the state space with $\fit(\sigma x)=\fit(x)$ for all $x$ and $\adj(\sigma x,\sigma y)\Rightarrow\adj(x,y)$. If $b$ is a strict local maximum, so is $\sigma b$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A neighbour $t\ne\sigma b$ pulls back to a neighbour $\sigma^{-1}t\ne b$ of $b$, whose fitness is below $\fit(b)$; transport along $\sigma$.
\end{proof}

Hence strict local maximality descends to the quotient by semantics-preserving renaming: no peak is an artefact of notation.

\begin{theorem}[Three-style metastability]\label{thm:threestyle}
Consider nine developments of a fixed corpus, three per style, with fitnesses $1,2,5$ (algebraic), $3,7,4$ (analytic), $6,2,9$ (combinatorial), and bounded refactorings connecting same-style developments. Then the three stylewise maximisers (fitness $5$, $7$, $9$) are three distinct strict local maxima, one per style; and $5<9$, $7<9$, so two of them are not global.
\end{theorem}

The landscape is genuinely multi-peaked: no path of small local improvements connects the algebraic peak to the strictly better combinatorial one. This is the formal image of two communities working on the same theorems with incompatible methodologies, each correctly observing that every small step towards the other makes matters worse.

\section{No universal maximum without resource normalisation}\label{sec:unbounded}

All preceding comparisons hold a corpus fixed. Dropping that hypothesis destroys the theory.

\begin{definition}
A \emph{theory language} consists of a type of developments with functions $\countf$ and $\len$ into $\mathbb{N}$, a semantics map, a marginal-cost function $\marg:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$, and an inflation operator $\ext(T,n)$ with
$\countf(\ext(T,n))=\countf(T)+n$; $\len(\ext(T,n))=\len(T)+\marg(n)$; \emph{conservativity} $\semant(\ext(T,n))=\semant(T)$; and \emph{sublinearity}: for every rational $c>0$ there is $N$ with $\marg(n)\le cn$ for $n\ge N$. Raw fitness is $\raw(T)=\countf(T)/\len(T)$.
\end{definition}

Inflation is not contrived: it is what happens when a general theorem is instantiated in many one-line special cases, each a stated and proved consequence adding no semantic content.

\begin{theorem}[Unboundedness and non-existence]\label{thm:noglobal}
If $\len(T_0)>0$ then for every $M\in\mathbb{Q}$ there is $n$ with $M<\raw(\ext(T_0,n))$. Consequently there is no $T$ with $\raw(U)\le\raw(T)$ for all $U$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
It suffices to treat $M>0$. Sublinearity at rate $c=1/(2M)$ gives $N$ with $\marg(n)\le n/(2M)$ for $n\ge N$; choose $k>2M\len(T_0)$ and take $n>\max(N,k)$. Then
\[
M\big(\len(T_0)+\marg(n)\big)\le M\len(T_0)+\tfrac n2<\tfrac n2+\tfrac n2=n\le \countf(T_0)+n,
\]
which is $M<\raw(\ext(T_0,n))$ after clearing the positive denominator. Any candidate maximum is then exceeded.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Semantic inertness]\label{thm:inert}
For every $M$ there is $n$ with $M<\raw(\ext(T_0,n))$ and $\semant(\ext(T_0,n))=\semant(T_0)$.
\end{theorem}

This is decisive: the unbounded family records no mathematical progress. Raw fitness diverges while the mathematics stands still, so unnormalised theorem-per-line is not merely maximum-free but meaningless as a measure of value.

\begin{theorem}[Sharp dichotomy]
Raw fitness attains a maximum on every nonempty finite comparison class and on no theory language of the above kind attains a global maximum.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}[Non-vacuity]
Take developments to be pairs $(\countf,\len)\in\mathbb{N}^2$, semantics constant, $\marg(n)=\lfloor\sqrt n\rfloor$ and $\ext((a,b),n)=(a+n,\,b+\lfloor\sqrt n\rfloor)$. Then $\lfloor\sqrt n\rfloor$ is sublinear: given $c>0$ pick $k>1/c$; for $n>k^2$ we have $\lfloor\sqrt n\rfloor\ge k$, hence $c\lfloor\sqrt n\rfloor>1$ and $\lfloor\sqrt n\rfloor\le c\lfloor\sqrt n\rfloor^{2}\le cn$. This language has no fitness champion.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms and applications}

\paragraph{Transitive closure and cost.} From $S=B$, iterate $S\leftarrow S\cup\bigcup_{i\in S}\deps(i)$ until $S$ stops growing (at most $|U|$ rounds; a worklist implementation runs in $O(|U|+E)$ for $E$ dependency edges) and return $\sum_{i\in S}\ell(i)$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:closure} the output is the canonical dependency-adjusted cost.

\paragraph{Champion by cost.} Compute closures for all competitors on a fixed corpus and return an argmin; by Theorem~\ref{thm:ordinal} this is a fitness champion.

\paragraph{Composition threshold.} Compute the intersection mass and compare with the adapter charge; by Theorem~\ref{thm:trichotomy} the sign of $S(T,U)-A$ is the sign of the fitness change.

\paragraph{Counting usable sub-libraries.} Decompose into weakly connected components and multiply the counts (Theorem~\ref{thm:mulsplit}); within a component, count order ideals by dynamic programming over a topological order. The extremes $2^n$ and $n+1$ bracket the answer and serve as sanity checks.

\paragraph{Minimum-cost cover.} With multiple routes the minimum is attained (Theorem~\ref{thm:mincover}), but by Theorem~\ref{thm:tworoutes} no closure-based procedure can be exact; the natural heuristic closes the union of the cheapest single route per statement.

\medskip

These give directly actionable criteria: Theorem~\ref{thm:reuse} quantifies the break-even point of factoring out a core ($(k-1)$ wasted copies); Theorem~\ref{thm:trichotomy} reduces a merger decision to one measurement; Theorem~\ref{thm:extremes} yields a modularity audit; Theorem~\ref{thm:valley} predicts guaranteed transient bloat during interface migrations; Theorem~\ref{thm:threestyle} recasts methodological pluralism as metastability rather than error; and Theorem~\ref{thm:inert} warns that any ``results per unit effort'' metric applied without a fixed corpus can be driven to infinity while producing no new mathematics.

\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{Accounting is easy; choice is hard.} Every exact result above --- closure minimality, the merge identity, the reuse identity, the composition threshold, the counts $2^n$ and $n+1$ --- is an accounting result determining what the numbers must be. Every difficulty concerns \emph{choice}: which proof route, which interface, which style. The boundary between the tractable and the intractable here is the boundary between bookkeeping and decision.

\paragraph{Canonicity is fragile; existence is robust.} One extra proof route destroys the canonical champion, yet the minimum-cost cover survives. What is lost is not the answer but the method: a closure computation becomes a combinatorial optimisation.

\paragraph{Normalisation is the whole game.} Theorems~\ref{thm:finmax} and~\ref{thm:noglobal} bracket the subject. Between them, ``there is a best theory'' is neither true nor false in general; it is true exactly to the extent that the comparison class has been normalised.

\paragraph{Limitations.} Source length is a crude proxy for cost, charging a hundred routine lines as much as a hundred subtle ones; theorem counts are a crude proxy for value, handled only by fixing a corpus. The adapter law of Theorem~\ref{thm:valley} is a hypothesis, not a theorem: the valley is derived \emph{given} the law, and validating it is empirical work. The multi-route model treats route sets as given, whereas discovering a cheap route is itself the expensive activity.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Canonicity gap]
With $r\ge2$ alternative routes per statement, deciding whether a corpus admits a covering library of dependency-adjusted cost $\le B$ is NP-complete, and the gap between the canonical-closure heuristic and the true optimum is $\Theta(\log|\text{corpus}|)$ in the worst case.
\end{conjecture}
Theorem~\ref{thm:tworoutes} is the exact obstruction: two cost-equal covering closures with empty intersection, so the covering problem loses its least element as soon as routes branch, leaving a weighted set-cover instance in disguise. Existence is not at risk (Theorem~\ref{thm:mincover}), so the open content is precisely the complexity of finding the optimum.

\begin{conjecture}[Sharp reuse threshold for realistic cost models]
For any subadditive adapter cost $A(s)\le c\,s^{\theta}$ with $\theta<1$, composing $k$ libraries of shared density $\rho$ increases fitness for all $k\ge k_0(\rho,\theta)$ with $k_0=O(\rho^{-1/(1-\theta)})$, and this exponent is optimal.
\end{conjecture}
The exact identity of Theorem~\ref{thm:reuse} makes the saving grow linearly in $k$ while a subadditive adapter grows sublinearly, so the crossing point is fixed by a single exponent rather than by the libraries' contents.

\begin{conjecture}[Universal adapter valleys]
In any real corpus the adapter law holds with $\alpha\ge1/4$ and endpoint efficiency $\beta\le1/10$, so every semantics-preserving migration across an interface boundary incurs a relative overshoot of at least $(1/4-1/10)/(11/10)=3/22$.
\end{conjecture}
Theorem~\ref{thm:crossing} reduces this to measuring a single class of cross-interface transitions.

\begin{conjecture}[Dependency-adjusted global champion, empirical form]
Among developments proving an entire fixed corpus, a mature shared library maximises dependency-adjusted fitness.
\end{conjecture}
Theorems~\ref{thm:sharedchamp} and~\ref{thm:globalchamp} identify exactly what remains empirical: a fixed comparison class, a reproducible cost model, and complete theorem-coverage measurements.

\begin{conjecture}[Three-style metastability, empirical form]
In a migration graph built from bounded refactorings of a real corpus, algebraic, analytic and combinatorial developments each contain a distinct strict local maximum after quotienting by semantics-preserving renaming.
\end{conjecture}
Theorem~\ref{thm:stylecentre} separates the two required conditions --- stylewise efficiency and rarely-crossing neighbourhoods --- making each independently measurable.

Finally, replacing source length by a cost model sensitive to conceptual depth leaves all ordinal results of \S\ref{sec:ordinal} intact, since they use only positivity; which of \S\S\ref{sec:reuse}--\ref{sec:counts} survive a non-additive cost is open.

\section{Conclusion}

Fixing a finite corpus, a source-length function and a transitive dependency closure charged exactly once produces a model in which the folklore of mathematical library design becomes provable or refutable. The shared core is the champion --- canonically for a fixed proof system, only up to cost ties otherwise; reuse saves exactly $(k-1)$ copies of the core; composition pays exactly when the adapter is cheaper than the shared mass; usable sub-libraries number between $n+1$ and $2^n$ with independent parts multiplying exactly; interface migrations must climb a valley of guaranteed relative depth $(\alpha-\beta)/(1+\beta)$; methodological styles can be genuinely metastable; and without normalisation there is no champion at all, because theorem-per-line can be inflated arbitrarily without proving anything new.

A best theory exists only relative to a fixed question. Ask what mathematics is \emph{for}, and the landscape has peaks. Ask only how much of it there is, and the landscape has no summit --- only an infinite, empty ascent.

\end{document}

